# ZM-knowledge-first — recall before you read

## Applies only when ZM is available — verified, stated, never assumed

- Active ONLY if the session exposes zero-memory tools (`mcp__zero-memory__*`). If they are
  absent (a fork/clone without a running ZM server), this rule is a **no-op** — proceed
  normally, do not attempt ZM calls, do not wait. ZM is an optional augmentation, never a hard
  dependency of this repo. (For consumer projects, this rule belongs in the user-global
  `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, not a committed per-repo rule — see `docs/getting-started/claude-code.mdx`.)
- **Verify absence before concluding it.** Tool lists can be deferred/lazily loaded — not
  seeing `mcp__zero-memory__*` in the visible tool list is NOT proof of absence. Actively
  check once per session (e.g. a tool-search for `zero-memory`) before deciding ZM is
  unavailable.
- **An empty check is NOT conclusive.** A tool-search that returns "no match" at task start is
  inconclusive: MCP/deferred tools frequently register a few calls into the session and surface
  only in a later tool round. Do not lock in "absent" from one empty check — treat it as "not
  yet visible", re-verify after the next tool round, and the moment `mcp__zero-memory__*`
  appears, switch to ZM-first for the rest of the task (including any sub-question already in
  flight).
- **The first reply carries a ZM-status line — in BOTH directions.** The violation is not only
  "silently grep when ZM is down"; it is equally "run one empty check and then edit/grep without
  a word about ZM". Before any non-ZM tool touches the task, the first reply states ONE of:
  (a) ZM visible → going through ZM first, with the `build_context`/`recall` call; or
  (b) ZM not yet visible → say so explicitly and fall through to code/docs. Silence in either
  direction is the process violation — the user must always know whether the answer was
  produced with memory or without it.

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 process gate. Runs at the START of any task or question, before any other
  knowledge lookup (code, docs, web, training memory).
- Sibling of `reuse-first-discovery.mdc`: that rule reuses existing CODE before writing new;
  this rule reuses existing KNOWLEDGE before deriving it again. Knowledge-reuse comes first,
  because a stored decision changes WHAT code you even look for.

## Use This When

- The moment a task, question, or investigation begins — feature, refactor, migration, bug,
  design choice, "why is it this way", "should it be X or Y".
- Before you grep the repo, open docs, search the web, or answer from training knowledge.

## Re-trigger IN-FLIGHT — not just at the start (the common miss)

The gate is not a one-time thing you satisfy once per task and then stop. It **re-fires every
time a NEW sub-question surfaces mid-task** — the moment your hand reaches for grep / glob /
Read (source or docs) / web to answer something you did not already have in context. The
failure mode this rule most often loses to is: query ZM at task start, then, three steps deep,
chase a fresh question straight into `grep` because "I'm already in the flow". Being deep in a
task does not exempt the next lookup — it IS the next lookup.

Concrete mid-task triggers (each one → ZM BEFORE the tool):
- **Diagnosing a bug / surprising behavior** → `recall(symptom, kinds:["gotcha"])` before
  forming a hypothesis. The gotcha is often already recorded from the moment it was first hit.
- **"Is this already decided — is there a prior decision/design for it?"** → `recall` before
  reading through docs or design notes. One `recall` returns the decision AND its linked
  context in a single call; grep finds one file and misses the web of related facts.
- **Reuse check before writing** (`reuse-first-discovery` sibling) → `recall` the capability
  before searching the code for it: a stored decision changes WHAT you grep for.
- **Picking a value / name / threshold / config shape** → `recall` for a prior decision-with-why
  before choosing from "what looks reasonable".

## Self-check gate (say it before the tool)

Before typing `grep` / `glob` / `Read`-of-source / a web search for a NEW question, answer:
"Have I asked ZM *this specific question*?" If no → `recall`/`build_context` first, then the
tool. This is a hard gate, not a nicety: reaching for code/docs/web on an un-ZM'd question is
the process violation, exactly like writing new code without searching for an existing one.

## Stop Here If

- Stop once ZM has been queried for the topic AND the relevant hits are in context (or you
  have confirmed by actually querying that ZM holds nothing on it). Only THEN fall through to
  code / docs / web.

## Core rule

- **ZM is the FIRST place you look, not a fallback.** Before code, docs, or web, query it.
  Answering a project question, or starting work, without having asked ZM is a process
  violation — the same class of miss as writing new code without searching for an existing one.
- **Task/topic start → `build_context(topic)`.** One call returns the relevant memories,
  entities, graph edges, and linked memories for the topic. Do this reflexively, like `git status`.
- **Point question / debugging → `recall(query)`.** Filter with `kinds` (e.g. `["gotcha"]`
  when chasing a bug, `["decision"]` when questioning a design) and `scopes` when you know the
  bounded context.
- **Before linking entities → `entities(query)`** to see what a name already resolves to.
- **Order of precedence when sources disagree:** a ZM `decision`/`convention` for THIS project
  outranks generic training knowledge and outranks what "looks reasonable". If ZM records a
  decision-with-why, follow it or explicitly challenge it — do not silently re-derive a
  different answer. (Example: a stored "config is instance-level, not per-user" decision must
  shape the schema you write, not be discovered after the fact.)

## Close the loop (so first-source stays worth it)

- ZM is only a useful first source if it is fed. The instant a durable fact surfaces — a
  decision WITH its why, a preference, a gotcha, or a convention not enforced by tooling —
  call `remember` NOW, at the moment of discovery, not at session end. One atomic fact per
  memory. See the memory policy in the ZM tool descriptions for what does and does not belong.

## Anti-patterns

- **Grepping code / reading docs / searching the web before asking ZM.** ZM is the pre-step,
  not the retrofit. When ZM is connected, the literal FIRST tool call of a task is
  `build_context`/`recall` — a task whose first call is grep/Read has already violated this.
- **Silently skipping ZM because the tools "aren't in the list".** Deferred tool loading makes
  the visible list unreliable; verify, and if truly absent — announce the fallback, don't
  bury it.
- **Running one empty check, then proceeding without a word about ZM.** An empty tool-search
  is not proof, and going straight to edit/grep with no ZM-status line in the first reply is
  the same violation as never checking — the user is left unable to tell if memory was used.
- **Treating the gate as one-and-done.** Querying ZM at task start, then grepping straight into
  every later sub-question because you're "already in the flow". The gate re-fires per lookup.
- **Searching through docs or design notes to find whether something is already decided.**
  `recall` first — it surfaces the decision-with-why and its linked context in one call; the
  file you land on is the tail end, found faster and richer through memory.
- **Debugging from a fresh hypothesis without `recall`-ing the symptom.** Gotchas are stored at
  the instant of first discovery; the bug in front of you may already be solved.
- **Answering a project question from training memory** when ZM holds a project-specific
  decision or gotcha on it.
- **Re-deriving a decision that is already stored** — and, worse, deriving a *different* one,
  silently contradicting a recorded decision-with-why.
- **Waiting for the user to say "check ZM first."** The query is YOUR default obligation;
  needing to be told is the failure this rule exists to remove.
- **Consuming without feeding:** discovering a durable fact and moving on without `remember`.
